# Batch 4 (2026-10-16): Africa/Asia/Americas/Oceania history, deep time, myths, UFO cases, hoaxes, stories proven true
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
SAT='Sceptical view'
E=[
# --- part 1: Africa & Asia history / archaeology
X('lalibela','Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela',H,'confirmed',2,1181,['archaeological','textual','oral tradition'],
 'Eleven monolithic churches carved down into the rock in the Ethiopian Highlands, traditionally attributed to the Zagwe king Gebre Meskel Lalibela (r. c. 1181–1221) as a "New Jerusalem". Many archaeologists think they took shape over several construction phases. UNESCO World Heritage Site (1978) and still an active pilgrimage centre.',
 [('Built with angelic help (tradition)','Local tradition, recorded by 16th-century Portuguese visitors, attributes the churches to angels working alongside human builders; this is a religious tradition, not a testable claim.'),
  ('Older Aksumite origin','Archaeologist David Phillipson proposed that some structures were first cut c. 600–800 CE as fortifications or palaces and only later converted to churches; others dispute this dating.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/18|Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela (UNESCO)|institution','wiki:Rock-Hewn_Churches,_Lalibela','wiki:Lalibela'],'Rock-Hewn_Churches,_Lalibela',end=1221,
 dateNote='Traditional date = reign of King Lalibela (c. 1181–1221); some parts may be older'),
X('kilwa','Kilwa Kisiwani and the Swahili coast',H,'confirmed',2,1100,['archaeological','textual'],
 'Island city off southern Tanzania that led the Kilwa Sultanate, a Swahili trading state whose influence peaked in the 13th–15th centuries. Kilwa minted its own coins (found as far inland as Great Zimbabwe); Ibn Battuta praised it c. 1331; the Portuguese sacked it in 1505. UNESCO World Heritage Site.',
 [('Founding tradition','The Kilwa Chronicle tells of a founding by a Persian (Shirazi) prince; historians debate how far this reflects actual migration versus later prestige claims, noting little evidence of Shirazi Shia Islam on the coast.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144|Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Songo Mnara (UNESCO)|institution','wiki:Kilwa_Kisiwani','wiki:Kilwa_Sultanate'],'Kilwa_Kisiwani',end=1505),
X('timbuktu-manuscripts','Timbuktu manuscripts',H,'confirmed',2,1300,['textual'],
 'A vast, still uncounted body of manuscripts (late 13th to early 20th centuries; one project alone has photographed over 150,000) on law, religion, astronomy, medicine, history and more, kept for generations in Timbuktu family libraries in Mali. In 2012–13, during the Northern Mali conflict, 4,203 were burned or stolen, while families and librarians smuggled most of the rest to Bamako; large digitisation projects followed.',
 [('Hidden knowledge angle','Because most manuscripts remain uncatalogued and unstudied, their full content is unknown; claims about what they might reveal are speculative until they are read and published.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/119|Timbuktu (UNESCO)|institution','wiki:Timbuktu_manuscripts'],'Timbuktu_manuscripts',end=2013,
 dateNote='Manuscripts date from the late 13th to the early 20th century; rescue 2012–13'),
X('ishango-bone','Ishango bone',H,'confirmed',2,-18000,['archaeological'],
 'Engraved bone tool (animal species undetermined) found in 1950 at Ishango, DR Congo, by Jean de Heinzelin; now usually dated to about 20,000 years ago. Its grouped notches are often described as one of the oldest possible mathematical artefacts; older notched bones exist, such as the c. 42,000-year-old Lebombo bone from southern Africa.',
 [('Arithmetic / prime numbers','De Heinzelin and later writers suggested the notch groups show deliberate arithmetic, and one column has been read as the primes between 10 and 20 (speculative; may be coincidence).'),
  ('Lunar calendar','Some researchers have proposed the marks track lunar phases (speculative).'),
  (SAT,'The marks could be tallies or simply grip-improving scratches; dating is also debated because volcanic activity disturbed carbon ratios.')],
 ['wiki:Ishango_bone','wiki:Lebombo_bone'],'Ishango_bone',dateNote='Usually dated c. 20,000 years ago; dating debated'),
X('prester-john','Prester John: the lost Christian king',M,'myth-legend',2,1145,['textual'],
 'Medieval European legend of a rich Christian priest-king ruling a lost realm "in the Indies". It is first recorded by Otto of Freising (1145), and a widely copied Letter of Prester John (c. 1165, probably a Western forgery) spread it. The legend was attached first to Central Asia and later to Ethiopia, whose rulers never used the title.',
 [('Real kernel','Scholars link the legend to real but distant Christians (Church of the East converts such as the Kerait of Central Asia, Christian Ethiopia), and the 1145 report to the Qara Khitai victory over the Seljuks at Qatwan in 1141.'),
  ('Propaganda','Some historians read it partly as crusade-era wishful thinking or propaganda about a powerful eastern ally.')],
 ['wiki:Prester_John'],'Prester_John',end=1700,
 changed='12th c.: placed in Asia. c. 1250 onward: increasingly identified with Ethiopia. 17th c.: Hiob Ludolf shows no native Ethiopian link and the search fades.'),
X('qin-mausoleum','Mausoleum of Qin Shi Huang (mercury rivers)',H,'confirmed',1,-246,['archaeological','textual','geochemical'],
 'Tomb complex of China\'s first emperor near Xi\'an, built 246–208 BCE under a 76 m mound and guarded by the Terracotta Army. The main tomb chamber has never been opened. Sima Qian wrote that it contained rivers and seas of mercury; soil surveys over the mound have measured anomalously high mercury levels.',
 [('Sima Qian confirmed','Many researchers see the soil mercury as support for Sima Qian\'s account of mercury rivers in the sealed chamber.'),
  (SAT,'Some argue the mercury readings may reflect local industrial pollution; only excavation could settle it, and archaeologists have delayed opening the tomb over preservation and safety concerns.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/441|Mausoleum of the First Qin Emperor (UNESCO)|institution','wiki:Mausoleum_of_Qin_Shi_Huang','wiki:Terracotta_Army'],'Terracotta_Army',end=-208),
X('borobudur','Borobudur',H,'confirmed',2,800,['archaeological'],
 'Huge 9th-century Mahayana Buddhist monument in Central Java, Indonesia, built as a stepped stupa with thousands of relief panels. It was later abandoned, possibly after volcanic eruptions and the shift of power to East Java (10th century) or after conversion to Islam, and lay under ash and jungle. Restored with UNESCO by 1983; World Heritage Site 1991.',
 [('Abandonment mystery','Why and when Borobudur was abandoned is still unresolved; volcanic, political and religious explanations are all proposed.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/592|Borobudur Temple Compounds (UNESCO)|institution','wiki:Borobudur'],'Borobudur',
 changed='1814: brought to European attention by Stamford Raffles after local Javanese reported it. 1975–82: major Indonesian–UNESCO restoration.'),
X('zheng-he','Zheng He\'s treasure voyages',H,'confirmed',2,1405,['textual','archaeological'],
 'Between 1405 and 1433 the Ming admiral Zheng He commanded seven large fleets across Southeast Asia and the Indian Ocean as far as Arabia and East Africa (a giraffe from Malindi was presented at court as a qilin). The voyages ended and Ming China turned inward.',
 [('China discovered America (1421 claim)','Gavin Menzies\'s 2002 book 1421 argued that Zheng He\'s fleets reached the Americas and circled the globe; historians reject this as pseudohistory for lack of evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Zheng_He','wiki:Ming_treasure_voyages','wiki:Gavin_Menzies'],'Zheng_He',end=1433),
X('homo-floresiensis','Homo floresiensis ("hobbits") and the Ebu Gogo',H,'confirmed',2,-100000,['archaeological','geological'],
 'A small-bodied archaic human species found in 2003 at Liang Bua cave on Flores, Indonesia. Revised dating (2016) puts the skeletal remains at about 100,000–60,000 years ago and stone tools to about 50,000 years ago, around when modern humans arrived. Its small size is usually explained by island dwarfism.',
 [('Pathology hypothesis','Some researchers argued the LB1 skeleton was a modern human with microcephaly or another disorder; later analyses mostly support a separate species.'),
  ('Folk memory (Ebu Gogo)','Flores folklore tells of small, hairy "Ebu Gogo" people; ethnologist Gregory Forth suggested such tales might preserve memories of real hominins. This has little support since the remains were dated to well before recent times (speculative).')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature02999','doi:10.1038/nature17179','wiki:Homo_floresiensis','wiki:Ebu_Gogo'],'Homo_floresiensis',end=-50000,
 changed='2004: initially thought to have survived until c. 12,000 years ago, fuelling Ebu Gogo speculation. 2016: re-dating pushes the remains back to 100–60 ka.'),
X('siberian-ice-maiden','Siberian Ice Maiden (Ukok princess)',H,'confirmed',3,-500,['archaeological','genetic'],
 'Naturally frozen mummy of a young woman of the Pazyryk culture (5th century BCE), found in 1993 by Natalia Polosmak on the Ukok Plateau in the Altai Republic, Siberia, with intact clothing, deer-style tattoos and sacrificed horses. Returned to Gorno-Altaisk in 2012.',
 [('Indigenous view','Many Altaians regard her as an ancestral figure (linked to the epic heroine Ochy-bala) and some have sought her reburial; further excavation on Ukok is barred at their request.'),
  ('Contested ancestry','Genetic claims about her European or Altaian ancestry became politically charged; scholars caution against using them to settle modern identity disputes.')],
 ['wiki:Siberian_Ice_Maiden','wiki:Pazyryk_culture'],'Siberian_Ice_Maiden'),
# --- part 2: Americas, Oceania, deep time, catastrophes
X('chaco-canyon','Chaco Canyon and the Sun Dagger',H,'confirmed',2,900,['archaeological','archaeoastronomical'],
 'Between about 900 and 1150 CE Chaco Canyon (New Mexico) was a major Ancestral Puebloan centre with great houses built from quarried sandstone and timber hauled long distances, linked by roads. On Fajada Butte, a "Sun Dagger" petroglyph where light passes through stone slabs onto spirals was recorded in 1977. UNESCO World Heritage Site.',
 [('Deliberate solar–lunar calendar','Researchers led by Anna Sofaer argue the Sun Dagger and building alignments track solstices, equinoxes and lunar cycles.'),
  (SAT,'Others think some alignments may be coincidental; the extent of planned astronomy is debated. In 1989 erosion from visitors shifted one slab, reducing the site\'s precision.')],
 ['url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/353|Chaco Culture (UNESCO)|institution','url:https://www.nps.gov/chcu/index.htm|Chaco Culture National Historical Park (US NPS)|institution','wiki:Chaco_Culture_National_Historical_Park','wiki:Fajada_Butte'],'Chaco_Culture_National_Historical_Park',end=1150),
X('kennewick-man','Kennewick Man (the Ancient One)',H,'confirmed',2,-6600,['archaeological','radiocarbon','genetic'],
 'Skeleton found in 1996 by the Columbia River in Washington State, radiocarbon-dated to about 8,400–8,700 years ago. A 2015 genome study found him closest to modern Native Americans. After nearly 20 years of legal dispute he was reburied by Columbia Basin tribes in 2017.',
 [('Early non-Native origin (superseded)','Early skull-shape analyses called him "Caucasoid", fuelling claims that ancient Europeans or Ainu-like people reached America first; ancient DNA did not support this.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature14625','wiki:Kennewick_Man'],'Kennewick_Man',
 changed='1996–2014: skull morphology suggests non-Native affinities. 2015: genome shows Native American ancestry. 2017: reburial.',
 dateNote='Lived c. 8,400–8,700 years ago; found 1996'),
X('polynesia-america-contact','Polynesian–American contact (Kon-Tiki to genomes)',H,'confirmed',2,1200,['genetic','botanical','experimental'],
 'In 1947 Thor Heyerdahl\'s Kon-Tiki raft sailed from Peru to Polynesia to argue that South Americans settled Polynesia. Most scholars rejected that model, since language and archaeology show Polynesians came from the west. A 2020 genome study found Native American ancestry in eastern Polynesians from a single contact around 1200 CE, and the sweet potato\'s spread also points to pre-Columbian contact.',
 [('Who travelled?','It is unresolved whether Polynesian voyagers reached South America and returned, or South Americans drifted west; the genetic signal fits either (open question).'),
  ('Heyerdahl vindicated?','Some see the genetics as partly vindicating Heyerdahl; most researchers note it shows brief contact, not South American settlement of Polynesia.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/s41586-020-2487-2','wiki:Kon-Tiki_expedition','wiki:Pre-Columbian_transoceanic_contact_theories'],'Kon-Tiki_expedition',
 changed='1947: Kon-Tiki popularises an American-origin theory. Late 20th c.: rejected in favour of a western origin. 2020: genomics confirms limited contact c. 1200 CE.'),
X('budj-bim','Budj Bim: eruption memory and eel traps',H,'confirmed',2,-34900,['geological','archaeological','oral tradition'],
 'Budj Bim (Mount Eccles), a volcano in south-western Victoria, Australia, was dated in 2020 by argon–argon methods to about 36,900 years ago. Gunditjmara creation stories tell of an ancestral being transforming into the volcano. The lava flow later hosted Gunditjmara eel-trap aquaculture, with one trap system carbon-dated to about 6,600 years ago (UNESCO World Heritage, 2019).',
 [('Oldest oral tradition?','Some researchers suggest Gunditjmara stories may preserve memory of eruptions in this volcanic province, possibly among the oldest surviving oral traditions; this is hard to test directly.'),
  (SAT,'Others caution that stories could describe later eruptions in the region, or be later interpretations of the volcanic landscape.')],
 ['doi:10.1130/G47166.1','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1577|Budj Bim Cultural Landscape (UNESCO)|institution','wiki:Budj_Bim'],'Budj_Bim_National_Park',
 dateNote='Eruption c. 36,900 years ago (Ar–Ar, 2020); eel traps from c. 6,600 years ago'),
X('cascadia-1700','1700 Cascadia earthquake and the "orphan tsunami"',H,'confirmed',1,1700,['geological','textual','oral tradition'],
 'On 26 January 1700 a magnitude ~8.7–9.2 megathrust earthquake ruptured about 1,000 km of the Cascadia subduction zone off western North America. Its tsunami reached Japan, where records of a wave with no felt earthquake allowed scientists in 1996 to date it to about 9 pm local time. Indigenous oral traditions from British Columbia to California describe a great quake and flood, e.g. the Huu-ay-aht account of Pachena Bay on a winter night.',
 [('Thunderbird and Whale','A widespread Northwest Coast motif of a battle between Thunderbird and Whale has been interpreted by some researchers as a mythic memory of earthquakes (interpretive).')],
 ['doi:10.1038/379246a0','wiki:1700_Cascadia_earthquake'],'1700_Cascadia_earthquake',
 changed='Before 1980s: Cascadia not thought capable of giant quakes. 1980s–90s: "ghost forests" (tree rings ending 1699) and Japanese records confirm the 1700 event and oral histories.'),
X('sn-1054','SN 1054: the Crab supernova "guest star"',H,'confirmed',2,1054,['textual','astronomical'],
 'Chinese astronomers recorded a "guest star" in July 1054 that remained visible until April 1056. Its remnant is the Crab Nebula, identified in the 20th century through the work of Lundmark, Mayall and Oort. The event is also mentioned in a later Japanese document and an Islamic-world source.',
 [('Ancestral Puebloan record','A pictograph near Peñasco Blanco in Chaco Canyon showing a star, crescent and hand is proposed to depict the supernova (plausible but unproven).'),
  ('Cahokia link','Some have speculated that the event influenced the rise or building at Cahokia (speculative).')],
 ['wiki:SN_1054','wiki:Crab_Nebula'],'SN_1054',end=1056),
X('volcanic-winter-536','Volcanic winter of 536 ("worst year to be alive")',H,'confirmed',2,536,['ice core','tree ring','textual'],
 'From 536 CE, at least three volcanic eruptions of uncertain location loaded the atmosphere with sulfate aerosols. Procopius wrote that the sun gave light "without brightness"; Northern Hemisphere summers cooled by up to ~2.5 °C, crops failed, and the Late Antique Little Ice Age (536–660) followed alongside the Plague of Justinian. Ice-core and tree-ring work published in 2015 helped fix the timing.',
 [('Impact or comet','Dallas Abbott and colleagues reported nickel- and copper-bearing spherules in an ice core, suggesting an impact may have contributed; most researchers emphasise volcanism (minority view).'),
  ('Myth echoes','Some writers link the darkness to later legends (e.g. Fimbulwinter before Ragnarök or Arthurian dates); such links are speculative.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/nature14565','wiki:Volcanic_winter_of_536','wiki:Late_Antique_Little_Ice_Age'],'Late_Antique_Little_Ice_Age',end=660),
X('giant-impact-moon','Giant impact forms the Moon (Theia)',H,'hypothesis',1,-4.5e9,['geochemical','computer modelling'],
 'The leading explanation for the Moon\'s origin: about 4.5 billion years ago, 20–100 million years after the Solar System formed, a Mars-sized body (often called Theia) collided with the proto-Earth, and the debris re-accreted as the Moon. Proposed in 1946 (Daly) and revived in 1974–75 (Hartmann and Davis), it was tested against Apollo-sample data and computer simulations.',
 [('Isotope puzzle','Lunar and Earth rocks are almost identical isotopically, which some models struggle to explain; alternatives include a high-energy impact, a synestia, or multiple smaller impacts.'),
  ('Other models','Older ideas such as capture or fission are now largely out of favour.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/35089010','wiki:Giant-impact_hypothesis'],'Giant-impact_hypothesis',
 changed='1946: Daly proposes it. 1974–75: revived (Hartmann & Davis); Apollo-era data debated. 2001: high-resolution simulations (Canup & Asphaug).'),
X('great-dying','The Great Dying (end-Permian extinction)',H,'confirmed',1,-251.9e6,['geological','paleontological','radiometric'],
 'The most severe mass extinction known, about 251.9 million years ago: roughly 81% of marine species and 70% of land vertebrate species vanished. High-precision dating (2014) puts the main pulse at about 60,000 years. The consensus cause is Siberian Traps flood-basalt eruptions, which drove warming, ocean acidification and oxygen-starved seas.',
 [('Impact contribution','Some researchers have proposed an asteroid impact played a role, but no accepted crater or impact signature has been confirmed (minority view).')],
 ['doi:10.1073/pnas.1317692111','wiki:Permian–Triassic_extinction_event'],'Siberian_Traps',end=-251.88e6),
X('draupner-wave','Draupner wave: sailors\' "freak waves" proven',H,'confirmed',2,1995,['instrumental'],
 'On 1 January 1995, a laser sensor on the Draupner gas platform in the North Sea measured a single 25.6 m wave, far larger than its surroundings. It was the first instrumental record of a rogue wave. For a long time sailors\' reports of such waves had been treated as exaggeration because standard models made them vanishingly rare.',
 [],['wiki:Draupner_wave','wiki:Rogue_wave'],'Draupner_wave',
 changed='Before 1995: rogue waves widely regarded as sailors\' tales. 1995: first measured. 2000s: satellite surveys show they are more common than models predicted.'),
# --- part 3: myths & legends of the world
X('anansi','Anansi the spider',M,'myth-legend',2,1600,['oral tradition'],
 'Anansi is a spider trickster in Akan (Ghana) religion and folklore, associated with stories, wisdom and cunning; in one well-known tale he buys all stories from the sky god Nyame. Through the Atlantic slave trade, Anansi stories spread to the Caribbean and the Americas, where they remain widely told.',
 [],['wiki:Anansi'],'Anansi',dateNote='Living oral tradition; date schematic (spread with the Atlantic slave trade)'),
X('rainbow-serpent','The Rainbow Serpent',M,'myth-legend',1,-5000,['oral tradition','rock art'],
 'A creator being found, under many names, across many Aboriginal Australian cultures (e.g. Yurlunggur among the Yolngu of Arnhem Land). It shapes land and waterways and gives law, but can bring floods and chaos when disturbed. It appears widely in rock art.',
 [('Environmental memory','Some researchers relate Rainbow Serpent imagery to seasonal monsoon floods or to post-glacial sea-level rise; these are interpretive readings.')],
 ['wiki:Rainbow_Serpent'],'Rainbow_Serpent',dateNote='Living oral tradition; date is schematic for timeline placement'),
X('maui','Māui the trickster demigod',M,'myth-legend',2,1300,['oral tradition'],
 'Across Polynesia, Māui is a culture hero and trickster who steals fire, slows the sun with snares, and fishes up land. In Māori tradition the North Island of New Zealand is Te Ika-a-Māui, "the fish of Māui", hauled up with a hook made from his grandmother\'s jawbone.',
 [('Island-finding memory','Some writers read "fishing up islands" as a poetic memory of voyagers discovering new islands (interpretive).')],
 ['wiki:Māui_(Māori_mythology)','wiki:Māui_(mythology)'],'Māui_(Māori_mythology)',dateNote='Pan-Polynesian tradition; date schematic (Aotearoa settlement c. 1300)'),
X('pele','Pele, goddess of Kīlauea',M,'myth-legend',2,1410,['oral tradition','geological'],
 'In Hawaiian religion Pele is the goddess of volcanoes and fire, said to have voyaged from Kahiki (Tahiti) and to live in Halemaʻumaʻu crater at Kīlauea. Volcanologists dated a major Kīlauea eruption around 1410, the first mentioned in Native Hawaiian oral history, with the 60-year ʻAilāʻau eruption ending around 1470.',
 [('Chants as eruption records','A 2008 study by volcanologists argued that Pele and Hiʻiaka traditions describe about 400 years of real Kīlauea activity, including major lava flows and summit collapse.'),
  ('Living deity','For many Hawaiians Pele is a living presence, not only a symbol; this is a religious view rather than a testable claim.')],
 ['doi:10.1016/j.jvolgeores.2008.01.033','wiki:Pele_(deity)','wiki:Kīlauea'],'Halemaʻumaʻu',dateNote='Tradition older; oral history linked to eruptions from c. 1410'),
X('five-suns','The Five Suns (Aztec world ages)',M,'myth-legend',1,1500,['textual','iconographic'],
 'In Nahua and Aztec belief the world has been created and destroyed four times; each age, or "Sun", ended in catastrophe. In one version the fourth sun, ruled by the water goddess Chalchiuhtlicue, ended in a great flood that turned people into fish. We live in the Fifth Sun, fated to end in earthquakes. The Aztec Sun Stone is commonly interpreted as depicting the five suns.',
 [('Memory of real catastrophes','Some writers link the world-ages to real floods, eruptions or impacts (speculative).'),
  ('Cross-cultural pattern','Cyclic world ages also appear in Hindu yugas, Hesiod\'s ages and Maya creation stories; diffusion versus independent invention is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Five_Suns','wiki:Aztec_sun_stone'],'Aztec_sun_stone',dateNote='Late Postclassic Aztec form; older Mesoamerican roots'),
X('el-dorado','El Dorado and the Muisca raft',M,'myth-legend',1,1537,['textual','archaeological'],
 'The legend began in 16th-century Spanish accounts of a "Golden One", a Muisca ruler who covered himself in gold dust and washed it off in a sacred lake, linked to Lake Guatavita in Colombia. It grew into the myth of a lost city of gold that drove many failed expeditions. In 1969 a gold Muisca votive raft showing a chief on a raft was found in a cave near Pasca.',
 [('Kernel of truth','Many scholars consider the Guatavita offering ceremony real, so the legend may be a story with a true core that was exaggerated into a lost golden city.')],
 ['wiki:El_Dorado','wiki:Muisca_raft'],'Muisca_raft',
 changed='16th–18th c.: hunted as a city of gold. 1969: Muisca raft supports the ritual behind the legend.',dateNote='Spanish reach Muisca lands 1537; raft found 1969'),
X('nuwa','Nüwa mends the sky',M,'myth-legend',2,-139,['textual'],
 'In Chinese mythology Nüwa is a mother goddess who moulded humans from clay. In the Huainanzi (2nd century BCE), the pillars of Heaven break, the sky collapses and floods spread, and Nüwa patches the sky with five-coloured stones and props it up with a giant turtle\'s legs.',
 [('Sky catastrophe memory','Some writers read the collapsing sky and flood as a memory of a cosmic impact or great flood (speculative).')],
 ['wiki:Nüwa'],'Nüwa',dateNote='Huainanzi compiled c. 139 BCE; tradition older'),
X('bakunawa','Bakunawa, the moon-eating serpent',M,'myth-legend',3,1500,['oral tradition'],
 'In Philippine mythology the Bakunawa is a giant sea or sky serpent that rises to swallow the moon, causing eclipses. Its movements were used as a calendar system by babaylan priestesses. Through Indian influence it was syncretised with the Nāga and the eclipse deities Rahu and Ketu.',
 [('Cross-cultural pattern','Eclipse-devouring creatures also appear in Hindu (Rahu), Norse (sky wolves) and Chinese (celestial dog) traditions.')],
 ['wiki:Bakunawa'],'Bakunawa',dateNote='Pre-colonial oral tradition; date schematic'),
X('naga-fireballs','Naga fireballs of the Mekong',M,'disputed',2,1990,['eyewitness','video'],
 'Glowing reddish balls are reported to rise from the Mekong River in Thailand and Laos, especially around the end of Buddhist Lent (Wan Ok Phansa) in October. Locally they are attributed to the phaya nak, a giant serpent living in the river, and the event draws large crowds.',
 [('Natural gas','One hypothesis is ignition of phosphine or methane from river sediments; critics say such gas would not ignite or stay lit as described.'),
  ('Human-made','A Thai biologist analysing footage concluded the lights were flares fired from the far bank; others dispute this.'),
  ('Supernatural','Many locals see the Naga as the true source, a religious view that cannot be tested.')],
 ['wiki:Naga_fireball'],'Naga_fireball',dateNote='Lights said to be centuries old; mass public events since c. 1990s'),
X('kumari-kandam','Kumari Kandam (lost Tamil continent)',M,'myth-legend',2,800,['textual'],
 'Tamil literary tradition tells of Pandyan lands "seized by the ocean" (kadalkol), including the cities of the first two Tamil Sangams. Twentieth-century Tamil revivalists merged this with the European Lemuria idea, picturing a sunken Tamil continent in the Indian Ocean called Kumari Kandam.',
 [('Sea-level memory','Some suggest the stories echo real coastal losses: post-glacial sea-level rise or later storms and tsunamis, such as the flood that Manimekalai says destroyed Kaverippumpattinam (speculative to partly plausible).'),
  ('Geology','Plate tectonics rules out a sunken continent in the Indian Ocean during human times.')],
 ['wiki:Kumari_Kandam'],'Kumari_Kandam',
 dateNote='Kadalkol story in an early-medieval commentary; earlier hints in Sangam verses; "Kumari Kandam" concept 20th c.'),
# --- part 4: UFOs, anomalies, hoaxes, stories proven true
X('nuremberg-1561','1561 celestial phenomenon over Nuremberg',H,'disputed',2,1561,['textual','woodcut'],
 'A broadsheet by Hans Glaser reports that at dawn on 14 April 1561 many people in Nuremberg saw crosses, spheres, crescents and cylinders in an apparent "battle" in the sky out of the sun, followed by a large black triangular object and spheres falling to earth in smoke.',
 [('Atmospheric optics','Sceptics and many historians interpret it as a vivid sun-dog or halo display, described through the religious-omen conventions of the time.'),
  ('UFO interpretation','Ufologists have proposed that observers saw extraterrestrial craft (speculative).')],
 ['wiki:1561_celestial_phenomenon_over_Nuremberg'],'1561_celestial_phenomenon_over_Nuremberg'),
X('tehran-1976','1976 Tehran UFO incident',H,'disputed',2,1976,['eyewitness','radar','documentary'],
 'On 19 September 1976, civilians and military personnel over Tehran saw a bright object, also tracked on radar. Two Imperial Iranian Air Force F-4 jets reported losing instruments and communications as they approached it, and one reported a temporary weapons failure. A US military report on the case was distributed to the DIA, CIA, State Department and White House.',
 [(SAT,'Philip Klass and James Oberg argued the pilots initially saw a bright astronomical object (probably Jupiter) and that the failures came from equipment faults and stress.'),
  ('Genuine anomaly','Ufologists regard it as one of the best multi-witness, radar-supported cases.')],
 ['wiki:1976_Tehran_UFO_incident'],None),
X('colares-1977','Colares "chupa-chupa" lights and Operação Prato',H,'disputed',3,1977,['eyewitness','documentary'],
 'In 1977 residents of Colares island (Pará, Brazil) reported lights in the sky that they said injured people, calling them "chupa-chupa". The Brazilian Air Force ran an investigation, Operação Prato (1977–78), led by Captain Uyrangê Hollanda; it closed without officially finding unusual phenomena, and its documents are now in the National Archives.',
 [('Real phenomenon','In a 1997 interview with ufologists, Hollanda recounted the operation\'s experiences; he died three months later, apparently by suicide, which drew conspiracy interest. Ufologists treat the case as significant.'),
  (SAT,'Others suggest mass panic, misidentified lights and medical conditions explain the reports.')],
 ['wiki:Operação_Prato'],'Operação_Prato',end=1978),
X('valentich-1978','Disappearance of Frederick Valentich',H,'disputed',2,1978,['radio transcript'],
 'On 21 October 1978, 20-year-old pilot Frederick Valentich, flying a Cessna 182 over Bass Strait, Australia, told Melbourne air traffic control that an unknown aircraft was hovering above him and his engine was running rough, and said "it\'s not an aircraft". He and the plane were never found.',
 [(SAT,'Investigators considered disorientation (possibly flying upside down and seeing his own lights reflected in the water) or an accident; the Department of Transport could not determine the cause and presumed the loss fatal.'),
  ('UFO interpretation','Ufologists cite belated reports of UFO sightings that night (speculative).')],
 ['wiki:Disappearance_of_Frederick_Valentich'],'Disappearance_of_Frederick_Valentich'),
X('hessdalen-lights','Hessdalen lights',H,'confirmed',2,1981,['eyewitness','instrumental'],
 'Unexplained lights have been reported in the Hessdalen valley in central Norway since at least the 1930s, with a peak of 15–20 sightings a week in 1981–84. Project Hessdalen (from 1983) and later automated stations run by Østfold University College and Italian researchers have recorded them; the phenomenon itself is real and documented, but its cause is unknown.',
 [('Natural physics','Proposed explanations include combustion of airborne dust (possibly linked to local scandium deposits) or a dusty plasma formed by radon decay; no consensus.'),
  ('Unknown intelligence','Some ufologists suggest an intelligent or non-human origin (speculative).')],
 ['wiki:Hessdalen_lights'],'Hessdalen',dateNote='Reports since at least 1930s; peak 1981–84'),
X('aaro-2024','AARO historical UAP report (2024)',H,'confirmed',2,2024,['documentary'],
 'The Pentagon\'s All-domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO), set up in 2022, published a report in March 2024 on the historical record of US government UAP investigations from 1945. It found no evidence that any sighting involved extraterrestrial technology. It also revealed a proposed, never-approved DHS programme ("Kona Blue") to reverse-engineer any craft obtained, and rejected its advocates\' claims.',
 [('Cover-up claims','Whistle-blowers and some members of Congress maintain that crash-retrieval programmes exist and were withheld from AARO; no public evidence has confirmed this (disputed).'),
  ('Unresolved cases','A share of reported UAP cases remain unexplained because of insufficient data, which both sides cite.')],
 ['wiki:All-domain_Anomaly_Resolution_Office'],None),
X('cardiff-giant','Cardiff Giant',H,'debunked',2,1869,['documentary'],
 'A 3 m "petrified man" dug up on 16 October 1869 on William Newell\'s farm in Cardiff, New York, which became a paid attraction. It was carved from gypsum on the orders of tobacconist George Hull, reportedly after an argument about biblical giants; P. T. Barnum even exhibited a copy.',
 [],['wiki:Cardiff_Giant'],'Cardiff_Giant'),
X('fujimura-hoax','Japanese Paleolithic hoax (Fujimura)',H,'debunked',2,2000,['documentary','video'],
 'From the 1970s, amateur archaeologist Shinichi Fujimura "found" ever-older Lower and Middle Palaeolithic artefacts in Japan, some sites (e.g. Zazaragi) becoming national historic sites. On 5 November 2000 the Mainichi Shimbun published hidden-camera footage of him planting artefacts; he confessed, and reviews of his sites rewrote Japan\'s early prehistory.',
 [],['wiki:Japanese_Paleolithic_hoax'],None,
 changed='1970s–90s: Fujimura sites push Japanese occupation far back into the Lower Palaeolithic. 2000: exposed. 2003: Japanese Archaeological Association review confirms the hoax.'),
X('coelacanth','Coelacanth: the "extinct" fish found alive',H,'confirmed',2,1938,['biological'],
 'Coelacanths were known only as fossils and thought to have died out about 66 million years ago. On 22 December 1938, museum curator Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer spotted one in a fisherman\'s catch off the Chalumna River, South Africa; J. L. B. Smith described it as Latimeria. Populations were later found off the Comoros, and a second species in Indonesia (late 1990s).',
 [],['doi:10.1038/143455a0','wiki:Coelacanth'],'Coelacanth',
 changed='Before 1938: extinct since the Cretaceous. 1938: living specimen. 1990s: Indonesian species.'),
X('laigle-meteorites','L\'Aigle 1803: stones from the sky accepted',H,'confirmed',2,1803,['eyewitness','scientific investigation'],
 'Before 1803 many scientists dismissed reports of stones falling from the sky as superstition, despite Ernst Chladni\'s 1794 argument that they came from space. On 26 April 1803 more than 3,000 fragments fell on L\'Aigle, Normandy, and Jean-Baptiste Biot\'s investigation for the French Academy convinced scientists that meteorites are real.',
 [],['wiki:L\'Aigle_(meteorite)','wiki:Ernst_Chladni'],'L\'Aigle_(meteorite)',
 changed='Pre-1794: falling stones widely dismissed. 1794: Chladni. 1803: L\'Aigle settles the question.'),
X('lemuria-mu','Lemuria and Mu (lost continents)',M,'debunked',2,1864,['textual'],
 'Lemuria was proposed in 1864 by zoologist Philip Sclater as a sunken land bridge in the Indian Ocean to explain lemur distributions, and Ernst Haeckel even suggested it as humanity\'s homeland. Plate tectonics made it unnecessary. Occultists (Blavatsky, 1888) and writers such as Augustus Le Plongeon and James Churchward (Mu, Pacific) recast it as a lost civilisation.',
 [('Esoteric tradition','Theosophical and New Age groups still describe Lemuria/Mu as a spiritual ancestral homeland (belief rather than testable claim).'),
  ('Geology','Geologists state that a continent cannot sink in the time frames claimed; ocean-floor data show no sunken continent.')],
 ['wiki:Lemuria','wiki:Mu_(mythical_lost_continent)'],'Lemuria',
 changed='1864: scientific hypothesis. 1880s–1920s: occult and lost-civilisation versions. Mid-20th c.: plate tectonics retires the scientific idea.'),
]
